+\paragraph{How relief could shape transport.}
+The interpretation must account for both circuit-specific orderings.
+Terrain can organize slope lift, while uneven surface heating can generate
+thermal lift~\cite[Chapter~9]{faa_glider2024}.
+Following a sequence of ridges could introduce bends and detours that reduce
+horizontal advance on an open route. This is compatible with the smaller
+mountain $H$ among open flights, but does not establish the mechanism: accessible
+lift could also shorten search, and a long straight ridge could sustain
+persistent motion. These possibilities do not predict a universal sign for
+the altitude association.
+
+In particular, an explanation based only on mountain detours does not explain
+why the closed-route ordering is reversed. Closed circuits add a return
+constraint, whose effect over 10--10000 s can depend on circuit size, leg
+durations and turn geometry. For example, if lowland closed routes turn or
+return on shorter time scales than mountain closed routes, more increments
+in the fitted range could span changes of direction, reducing the effective
+$H$. This is a testable possibility, not a measured property of these flights.
+The downward bending of all four closed curves at long lags in
+Figure~\ref{fig:fixed-task} is compatible with return constraints, but does not
+identify why their altitude ordering differs from that of open routes.
+
+Distinguishing these explanations requires comparing circuit sizes and leg
+durations, separating triangles from out-and-return routes, and accounting
+for wind and the terrain actually crossed. The present curves establish the
+opposite conditional orderings, while their physical explanation remains open.
 
